1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Tongans saw — sailing
routes.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
For Tongans, astronomy was part of
navigation.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Their sky survives almost only in a
sailing guide written by high chief
Tukuʻaho in the 1890s.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
From Tonga, the Pole Star stays more
than 20° below the horizon.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Mid-November, 10 p.m., on Vavaʻu: a W
sits about 10° above the northern sea.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The guide says it is a hen, wings
spread, brooding over Tafahi island.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Which eight stars? Sources differ.
Stellarium guesses Cassiopeia.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A ring of small stars. Use it only when
it sits over ʻUvea like a headband.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Stellarium guesses Corona Borealis,
highest in Tonga’s north on July
evenings.

10
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
After dark, look northwest: the W of
Cassiopeia, Stellarium’s guess for
Tafahi’s Wing.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Ancient Arabia: a desert zoo in the sky.

12
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

